Sealing Officials (彌封官):
Chen Shaogong 陳紹功, Prefectural Judge (推官) of Guangzhou Prefecture (courtesy name Jiqing 及卿, a native of Jinjiang County, Fujian, and a metropolitan graduate of the gengchen year [1580]);
Weng Zhongyi 翁仲益, Superintendent (提舉) of the Guangdong Maritime Trade Supervisorate (courtesy name Shoufu 受甫, a native of Jinjiang County registered in Min County, Fujian, and a metropolitan graduate of the jiaxu year [1574]);
You Yinglong 游應龍, Magistrate (知縣) of Zengcheng County, Guangzhou Prefecture (courtesy name Tingcong 廷從, a native of Putian County, Fujian, and a metropolitan graduate of the gengchen year [1580]);
Feng Shengyu 馮生虞, Magistrate of Xiangshan County, Guangzhou Prefecture (courtesy name Deqing 德卿, a native of Dazu County, Sichuan, and a metropolitan graduate of the dingchou year [1577]);
Zhang Xigao 張希皐, Magistrate of Dianbai County, Gaozhou Prefecture (courtesy name Zhiqing 直卿, a native of Anlu County, Huguang, and a metropolitan graduate of the dingchou year [1577]).

Transcribing Officials (謄錄官):
Tang Minmin 唐民敏, Prefectural Judge of Zhaoqing Prefecture (courtesy name Xueqing 學卿, a native of Quanzhou, Guangxi, and a tribute scholar [貢士, i.e., provincial graduate] of the guiyou year [1573]);
Yuan Kui 袁奎, Magistrate of Xinhui County, Guangzhou Prefecture (courtesy name Wenqing 文卿, a native of Fengcheng County, Jiangxi, and a metropolitan graduate of the gengchen year [1580]);
Zhang Banghan 章邦翰, Magistrate of Chaoyang County, Chaozhou Prefecture (courtesy name Gongzuo 公佐, a native of Nanchang County, Jiangxi, and a metropolitan graduate of the gengchen year [1580]);
Tan Yizhao 譚一召, Magistrate of Suixi County, Leizhou Prefecture (courtesy name Zhongqing 忠卿, a native of Dayu County, Jiangxi, and a metropolitan graduate of the gengchen year [1580]).

Proofreading Officials (對讀官):
Chen Yangchan 陳楊產, Assistant Secretary (都事) of the Office of the Registrar (經歷司) of the Guangdong Provincial Administration Commission (courtesy name Yiqing 以清, a native of Fushun County, Sichuan, registered in Tongren Prefecture, Guizhou, and a metropolitan graduate of the jiaxu year [1574]);
Zhang Zuozhi 張佐治, Magistrate of Gaoming County, Zhaoqing Prefecture (courtesy name Sijin 思藎, a native of Pinghe County, Fujian, and a metropolitan graduate of the jiaxu year [1574]);
Huang Nanjin 黃南金, Magistrate of Guangning County, Zhaoqing Prefecture (courtesy name Shishuo 師說, a native of Nan'an County, Fujian, and a tribute scholar [貢士, i.e., provincial graduate] of the wuwu year [1558]);
Cai Zongzhou 蔡宗周, Magistrate of Xuwen County, Leizhou Prefecture (courtesy name Jingmian 敬冕, a native of Longxi County, Fujian, and a metropolitan graduate of the gengchen year [1580]).

Patrolling Officials (巡綽官):
Wang Shichen 汪世臣, Commander (指揮使) of the Shaozhou Independent Battalion (守禦千戶所) (courtesy name Guozuo 國佐, a native of Tongcheng County, Zhili)…

… His Majesty showing the imperial composition "Melody of theLuxuriant Orchid" (Yilan cao) to the Auxiliary Ministers [Seventh year of the Xuande reign (1432)].

Legal Judgments [Five items]
Rules on increasing or reducing punishments;
Lands of meritorious officials;
Gates of traveling palaces and encampments;
Recusal in hearing litigation;
Supplementary weaving of silk piece-goods.

Third Session

Policy Questions [Five items]
Question: Employing both civil and military arts as the method for enduring peace and stability—those who discuss the Way of the Ruler are generally able to speak of this. Do the civil and the military indeed depend upon each other? Since antiquity, the grand strategies of sovereigns, kings, and emperors, by which they ordered the state and alternated between tension and relaxation, have generally not departed from this; what was their principle like? From the Han and Wei dynasties onward, literary composition flourished, and rulers and ministers, superiors and inferiors, constantly vied with one another for preeminence in rhapsodies and panegyrics; meanwhile, the statutory institution of the Grand Military Review (dayue) in the Offices of Zhou (Zhouguan) was followed in later ages merely as a routine formality. Where did they stand with respect to the civil and the military? Can you also give a general account of this? Our August Emperor…
